## Summary

This is the **spring-petclinic-rest** codebase using the OfficeFloor framework. All duplicate detection is on **`POST /api/owners`** only. There is **no name-only duplicate check** — dedup is by **telephone** and **email**. The current checkpoint (`cp05`) concerns **case-insensitive** duplicate detection.

### Commit chain (from `/home/daniel/compare/officefloor/.git/worktrees/officefloor-just-solve-chain9/logs/HEAD`)
cp01 exact-duplicate → cp02 household-duplicate (same last name AND telephone) → cp03 telephone-unique → cp04 email-unique → **cp05 (current, checked out at `__preagent_cp05__`)**. So there is no "name-unique" checkpoint; the closest name-based rule is cp02 (last name + telephone), which was later subsumed by the telephone-unique rule.

## 1. Duplicate-detection production code

`/home/daniel/spring-petclinic-rest-long-degradation-test/work/202608011310/officefloor-just-solve-chain9/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RejectDuplicateOwner.java` — telephone dedup:
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) throws ConflictException {
    for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) {
        if (!Objects.equals(existing.getId(), owner.getId())
            && Objects.equals(owner.getTelephone(), existing.getTelephone())) {
            throw new ConflictException("An owner with the same telephone already exists");
        }
    }
}
```

`/home/daniel/spring-petclinic-rest-long-degradation-test/work/202608011310/officefloor-just-solve-chain9/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RejectDuplicateOwnerEmail.java` — email dedup (skips blank email):
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) throws ConflictException {
    String email = owner.getEmail();
    if (!StringUtils.hasText(email)) {
        return;
    }
    for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) {
        if (!Objects.equals(existing.getId(), owner.getId())
            && Objects.equals(email, existing.getEmail())) {
            throw new ConflictException("An owner with the same email already exists");
        }
    }
}
```

Both use **exact `Objects.equals`** comparison — no case-folding, no trimming, no normalization. Neither compares `firstName`/`lastName`.

### Wiring (YAML)
`/home/daniel/spring-petclinic-rest-long-degradation-test/work/202608011310/officefloor-just-solve-chain9/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` — pipeline order: `build → rejectDuplicate (telephone) → rejectDuplicateEmail → save → respond`. This is where a name-based `rejectDuplicate*` step would be inserted. (Note: no `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` dedup step exists — dedup is create-only.)

Related model helper (the only case-insensitive name comparison in `src/main`), NOT owner dedup: `/home/daniel/.../src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` lines 132-144, `getPet(String name, boolean ignoreNew)` lowercases both sides (`name = name.toLowerCase()` / `compName = compName.toLowerCase()`) to match a pet name within one owner.

## 2. Tests for duplicate behavior (all under `src/test/.../acceptance/`)

- **cp05 (current target)** `/home/daniel/.../src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/Cp05Tests.java` — "duplicate detection ignores letter case." `coreUpperCaseHouseholdDuplicate` and `functionalityLowerCaseHouseholdDuplicate` create an owner, then create a second with the **same lastName upper/lower-cased AND same telephone**, expecting **409 Conflict**. Its Javadoc explicitly notes it triggers the conflict via the shared **telephone** (not the name), because the base app's letters-only name pattern rejects padded/whitespace names with 400 before dedup, so it only asserts the case dimension.
- **cp02** `/home/daniel/.../acceptance/Cp02Tests.java` — rejects same lastName AND telephone (409); allows same lastName with different telephone (2xx).
- **cp03** `/home/daniel/.../acceptance/Cp03Tests.java` — telephone must be unique (409); distinct telephones allowed.
- **cp04** `/home/daniel/.../acceptance/Cp04Tests.java` — email optional; if present must be unique (409); missing email allowed; stored email readable.
- Base helpers: `/home/daniel/.../acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — `ownerNode()` builds a fully-unique owner; `uniqueLastName()` returns `"Sur"+letters(seq)`; generators keep names **letters-only** to satisfy the base validation pattern.

Controller-level tests also touch owners/vets but not name-dedup logic: `/home/daniel/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`, `/home/daniel/.../rest/controller/v1/VetRestControllerV1Tests.java`.

## 3. Normalization / case / whitespace

- **Production dedup does NO normalization** — telephone and email are compared with exact `Objects.equals`. No `toLowerCase`, `trim`, `strip`, `equalsIgnoreCase`, or `Locale` anywhere in the two `RejectDuplicate*` classes (confirmed by grep over `src/main`).
- The **only** normalization in `src/main` is `Owner.getPet(...)` lowercasing pet names (unrelated to owner dedup).
- The **cp05 spec expectation** (per `Cp05Tests` Javadoc) is that duplicate detection should be **case-insensitive**; whitespace normalization is mentioned in the spec but not asserted because the letters-only name pattern rejects padded names with 400 first.

## 4. Not present
No pet or vet duplicate-by-name detection exists. In `src/main/.../rest/function/vet` and `.../pet`, the only "duplicate" reference is `SpecialtyResolution.java` (avoids inserting duplicate specialty rows), unrelated to person/name dedup. No `name-unique` YAML or function class exists.

